    {
      "label": "Resonator instruments are sensitive to a periodic environmental or instrumental artifact (e.g., 5-day maintenance cycle, temperature swing, or digital sampling phase) that does not significantly affect pendulum instruments",
      "probability": 0.04,
      "rationale": "Resonators may simply be more susceptible to certain types of perturbation than pendulums. A 5-day cycle could reflect operational rhythm of the settlement (e.g., weekly calibration, supply delivery). This is a weaker version of the environmental coupling hypothesis, less specific but still plausible.",
      "evidenceFor": [
        "resonator instruments show stronger correlation than pendulum instruments"
      ],
      "evidenceAgainst": [
        "no settlement-wide 5-day rhythm has been reported",
        "all instruments show stable baselines and no level changes over 10 days"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "residual": 0.01,
  "summaryOfChange": "Previous review had no active hypotheses. Today's data reveal a single anomaly worthy of immediate investigation: resonator_lab and resonator_obs correlate at 0.561 (5-day offset), the only cross-instrument correlation exceeding its statistical threshold. This is unexpected and demands explanation. I have built four hypotheses ranked by explanatory power and prior plausibility: environmental/mechanical coupling of resonators (0.52), direct crosstalk (0.28), spurious correlation (0.15), and resonator-specific sensitivity to an unidentified 5-day cycle (0.04). The residual anomaly risk (0.01) is minimal; the question is now concrete. All other instruments remain within baseline expectations. My next priority is to collect post-baseline data (days 11+) to test whether the 5-day lag structure persists; if it does, I will investigate the physical environment and configuration of both resonator setups. Pendulum instruments will continue on schedule and may help rule out settlement-wide environmental confounds."
}
